  <p><strong>The company:</strong> stagewise (Y Combinator Summer 2025 batch), described by YC as an open-source frontend coding agent for production codebases, founded by Glenn Toews (CEO) and Julian Goetze (CTO), a team whose origin traces to the Founders Foundation in Germany. <strong>The move:</strong> they built a framework-independent toolbar that runs inside the browser on localhost, so a developer clicks and comments on real UI elements and directs an agent that edits the local codebase, with the source of the product being a single observation: the high-speed AI app-building experience, in their words, "vanished once moving into a real-world codebase." Their launch put it plainly, that once you move into a production-grade setup on your machine, "you lose the awesome experience of visually building your apps frontend with simple prompts." <strong>Why it worked:</strong> they refused to pretend one tool fits both jobs, so the product ships two explicit modes, temporary test changes for throwaway exploration and a connected real codebase for permanent edits, the exact boundary this lesson asks you to draw before your first prompt.</p>
